      ],
      "source": "https://villageofvalier.org/history",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The village’s name honors William Valier, the original landowner whose property became the town’s foundation.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Valier%2C_Illinois",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A short “Dinky” train ran through Valier from the 1920s until the 1950s, linking residents to neighboring towns.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        29,
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://villageofvalier.org/history",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Coal mining was the town’s lifeblood, with the local mine operating intermittently until it closed for good in 1960.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://villageofvalier.org/history",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Valier sits 9 miles west of Benton, the Franklin County seat, placing it in the heart of western Illinois’s rural landscape.",
